# gRPC server for GC communication
MC_GRPC_BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:50052
# Health endpoint
MC_HEALTH_BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:8081
# WebTransport server for client connections
MC_WEBTRANSPORT_BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:4433
# Deployment region (local for Kind)
MC_REGION=local
# GC service discovery via Kubernetes DNS
GC_GRPC_URL=http://gc-service.dark-tower:50051
# AC JWKS endpoint for JWT validation
AC_JWKS_URL=http://ac-service.dark-tower:8082/.well-known/jwks.json
# TLS certificate paths for WebTransport (mounted from mc-service-tls Secret)
MC_TLS_CERT_PATH=/etc/mc-tls/tls.crt
MC_TLS_KEY_PATH=/etc/mc-tls/tls.key
# ---- OpenTelemetry (R-55, task #6) ----
# MC's OTel SDK is wired into main.rs via init_otel (R-55), gated by an
# explicit boolean OTEL_ENABLED (NOT presence of OTLP_ENDPOINT). This is the
# prod base, so OTEL_ENABLED defaults to "false" here; the Kind overlay
# patches it to "true" for dev. Enabling requires OTEL_ENABLED=true PLUS the
# MC->collector egress NetworkPolicy rule on :4317 (now present) PLUS a
# reachable collector — init_otel probes eagerly and FAILS HARD at startup if
# the collector is unreachable (pod fails readiness; mirrors GC's task #26 /
# AC's task #25 exemplars).
OTEL_ENABLED=false
# OTLP-gRPC endpoint for the in-cluster OTel collector (R-59) — MC's OWN
# span-export endpoint. The http:// scheme is required (parsed via tonic
# Endpoint::from_shared — a bare host:port fails at init). Only consumed when
# OTEL_ENABLED=true. Namespace is dark-tower (NOT default).
OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel-collector.dark-tower:4317
# Head-sampling ratio in [0.0, 1.0]; init_otel is the authoritative validator.
OTEL_SAMPLE_RATE=1.0
# deployment.environment resource attribute (ADR-0011). NOTE: this prod base
# hardcodes "development"; prod OTel graduation MUST overlay-patch this so
# prod telemetry is not mistagged (same tracked caveat as GC's task #26 —
# see docs/TODO.md).
DEPLOYMENT_ENVIRONMENT=development
# ==========================================================================
# Client-facing media signalling (ADR-0036 §5, §6)
#
# EVERY KEY IN THIS BLOCK THAT AN IMAGE READS IS REQUIRED. mc-service reads
# them via ConfigError::MissingEnvVar with NO Rust default, so a missing key
# CrashLoopBackOffs the pod rather than silently reverting to a value nobody
# chose. That is deliberate: a Rust-defaulted key wired into mc-0 and
# forgotten on mc-1 passes `dt-guard env-config` green while the two MC
# instances behave differently. The documented default is the value below —
# this file is the source, not a constant in config.rs.
#
# The video and audio ENCODING parameters MC directs no longer live here: they
# are the shared `media-codec-profile` (infra/services/media-codec-profile/
# profile.env), which MH also reads. Only MC-only knobs belong in this file —
# including the one directive parameter MH has no use for, the minimum
# keyframe interval (`MC_VIDEO_MIN_KEYFRAME_INTERVAL_MS` below; the profile
# header's membership rule).
#
# ROLLOUT ORDERING. This ConfigMap is a `configMapGenerator` (ADR-0038 §2,
# content-addressed): a VALUE EDIT here renames the ConfigMap, changes both
# Deployments' pod templates, and ROLLS mc-0 AND mc-1 on the next apply of the
# environment root. It is never a hot toggle.
#
# THE COUPLED SET IS THREE ARTIFACTS, NOT TWO: this ConfigMap, BOTH
# Deployments, and the image. The Deployment is in the set because it carries
# the `configMapKeyRef` — it is what declares that a pod CONSUMES a key; this
# file only supplies the value. A key present here but unreferenced there is
# not consumed at all. Add a key (and its refs) with-or-before the image that
# reads it; remove a read one deploy before its key (the retirement banners
# below), never in the same deploy.
#
# See docs/runbooks/mc-deployment.md §Config-failure triage: a new Deployment
# against an old ConfigMap gives CreateContainerConfigError (empty logs,
# `describe` names the key), while a new image against an old pod template
# gives CrashLoopBackOff (`logs --previous` names the variable) — opposite
# first steps.
#
# (Corrected 2026-10-20: this block previously said ConfigMaps were listed
# under `resources:`, not `configMapGenerator:`, so editing this file did not
# roll the pods. ADR-0038 §2 made that false; every MC ConfigMap is generated.)
# ==========================================================================
# Per-kind receive-slot caps (story 3 R-1, R-26; ADR-0036 §6). Each kind has
# its own server-side cap, and a client declaring more slots of a kind than
# its cap is TRUNCATED to the cap: each excess slot is reported in the
# permanent `SLOT_STATE_UNAVAILABLE` state (proto/dark_tower/signaling/v1/
# signaling.proto), echoing the declared slot_id with no sender and no url —
# visible, never a silent participant, and NEVER A JOIN FAILURE. This
# supersedes story 2 R-1's reject-the-whole-declaration rule (and the retired
# TOTAL `MC_MAX_RECEIVE_SLOTS` below, which nothing reads any more).
#
# READ ONCE, AT STARTUP. A cap edit applies only through the MC restart its
# ConfigMap roll causes (every meeting on the pod reconnects and redeclares);
# MC never re-truncates a live declaration in place. Both caps are echoed to
# every client on JoinResponse (`max_audio_receive_slots`,
# `max_video_receive_slots`) from the same parsed values the gauge reads.
#
# Maximum AUDIO receive slots per client.
# THE VALUE ITSELF IS BOUNDED (bounds in crates/mc-service/src/config.rs, the
# per-kind range constants) and mc-service refuses to start outside them.
# The floor is not zero: an audio cap of 0 would mean no client can hear
# anyone — a silent total outage that reads as valid configuration. The
# ceiling keeps the property ADR-0036 §6 relies on this cap for
# ("resource-amplification-by-request becomes structurally impossible rather
# than rate-limited"); a cap whose own value is unbounded is not a cap.
# Enforced by: mc-service, in-process, when it truncates a receive declaration
# (at join and on every redeclaration).
# Where an operator sees it: `mc_media_receive_slot_cap{media_kind="audio"}`
# (the value the pod enforces), the `max_audio_receive_slots` field of MC's
# startup log, and truncated slots on
# `mc_media_slot_states_total{media_kind="audio",slot_state="unavailable"}`.
MC_MAX_AUDIO_RECEIVE_SLOTS=8
# Maximum VIDEO receive slots per client. Bounded like the audio cap (bounds
# in crates/mc-service/src/config.rs), EXCEPT that 0 is
# deliberately legal: it is the R-26 VIDEO-OFF LEVER.
#
# THE LEVER AND ITS BLAST RADIUS. Setting 0 truncates every video slot to
# unavailable, so no publisher is watched and no camera is directed to send;
# audio is unaffected. It applies to declarations made after the new MC pods
# are up. Because this ConfigMap is content-addressed it ROLLS BOTH MC PODS
# (one restart per pod's meetings) — it is not a hot toggle. It is deliberately
# NOT in `media-codec-profile`, so pulling it never rolls MH (an MH roll sheds
# every media session on the pod). The operator procedure, deploy order and
# rollback live in docs/runbooks/mc-deployment.md; this comment does not
# duplicate them.
# Enforced by: mc-service, in-process, when it truncates a receive declaration
# (at join and on every redeclaration).
# Where an operator sees it: `mc_media_receive_slot_cap{media_kind="video"}`
# and the `max_video_receive_slots` field of MC's startup log. A reading of 0
# on every MC pod confirms the lever applied.
MC_MAX_VIDEO_RECEIVE_SLOTS=4
# RETIRED — PENDING REMOVAL. Do not tune, do not "fix".
#
# Superseded by the per-kind caps above, `MC_MAX_AUDIO_RECEIVE_SLOTS` and
# `MC_MAX_VIDEO_RECEIVE_SLOTS`. This key is a TOTAL across kinds.
#
# WHAT IS TRUE NOW: NOTHING READS THIS KEY. Story 3 MC-A removed the read
# (and the whole-declaration rejection it enforced); editing it changes no
# behaviour and no series. `mc_media_receive_slot_cap{media_kind="audio"}` now
# reports `MC_MAX_AUDIO_RECEIVE_SLOTS`.
#
# WHY IT IS STILL HERE (two-deploy retirement): the read went one deploy before
# the key, so a rollback to the pre-MC-A image still finds it. Both refs
# (mc-0/mc-1-deployment.yaml) are `optional: true` so a later deploy can
# delete key and refs together without any reachable revision holding a hard
# ref to a missing key. Scheduled removal: docs/TODO.md §Media
# Path Obligations, "Delete the story-3 retired config keys".
MC_MAX_RECEIVE_SLOTS=8
# Maximum ACCEPTED receive-capability declarations per WebTransport
# connection. Bounds client-driven O(N) roster reads on the shared meeting
# actor: a client alternating between two valid declarations would otherwise
# drive one roster clone per message. Charged ONLY on acceptance — every
# rejection is decided without touching the meeting actor, so a client looping
# rejects cannot bypass it. Bounded at load (crates/mc-service/src/config.rs).
# 64 is generous for legitimate layout changes over a long session.
# Enforced by: mc-service, in-process, per connection.
# Where an operator sees it: mc_media_receive_capability_declarations_total
# {outcome="declaration_budget_exhausted"}.
MC_MAX_RECEIVE_CAPABILITY_DECLARATIONS=64
# Minimum interval, in milliseconds, between keyframes a video publisher
# produces (story 3 R-11, R-12, R-23; amendment 4). There is no keyframe
# INTERVAL: this bounds both request-driven keyframes and the publisher keying
# a target with no group in progress.
#
# MC-ONLY, BY THE PROFILE'S MEMBERSHIP RULE. MH's keyframe requests are
# event-driven and MH never reads this value, so it lives here, not in
# `media-codec-profile`: editing it rolls mc-0 and mc-1 only, never MH.
# (It was `MEDIA_VIDEO_MIN_KEYFRAME_INTERVAL_MS` in the profile until MC-A,
# deleted outright as amendment 4 directed — the one exception to the
# two-deploy retirement rule. The pre-MC-A mc-0/mc-1 revisions hold a HARD ref
# to it, which stays safe for `kubectl rollout undo` because undo returns to
# the previous hash-suffixed profile generation, retained (deploy.sh never
# prunes generations) and still holding the key. Its removal changed the
# profile's content hash, which rolled every MH once.)
#
# SERVER-CONFIGURED, NEVER CLIENT-REPORTED. MC carries it on every video send
# directive as `min_keyframe_interval_ms`; the client honours keyframe requests
# at most once per max(directed value, its compiled floor). A client-reported
# value would let a patched client suppress the keyframe-storm alert.
#
# BOUNDED: `MIN_KEYFRAME_INTERVAL_MS_FLOOR..=MIN_KEYFRAME_INTERVAL_MS_CEILING`
# in crates/mc-service/src/media_signaling/directive.rs (figures stated there,
# not here; the floor's comment there names its client-side counterpart).
# Enforced by: mc-service at config load, through `VideoEncoding`'s validating
# constructor. Required, no Rust default: a pod without the key crash-loops on
# MissingEnvVar (and both Deployments hold a hard ref, so the kubelet refuses
# first with CreateContainerConfigError).
# Where an operator sees it: `mc_media_video_keyframe_min_interval_seconds`
# (the value MC directs, in seconds) and the `video_min_keyframe_interval_ms`
# field of MC's startup log.
MC_VIDEO_MIN_KEYFRAME_INTERVAL_MS=500
# RETIRED — PENDING REMOVAL (all three MC_AUDIO_* keys). Do not tune.
#
# Superseded by `MEDIA_AUDIO_CODEC`, `MEDIA_AUDIO_MAX_BITRATE_BPS` and
# `MEDIA_AUDIO_FRAME_RATE_HZ` in the shared media-codec-profile
# (infra/services/media-codec-profile/profile.env), which carries their
# rationale — the MH buffer-latency coupling, the do-not-unify constants and
# the frame-rate bound — corrected for MH now READING those values.
#
# WHAT IS TRUE NOW: NOTHING READS THESE THREE. Story 3 MC-A removed the reads;
# the audio directive now carries the profile's `MEDIA_AUDIO_*` values. Editing
# them changes nothing — but keep them equal to the profile values while they
# exist, because a rollback to the pre-MC-A image directs these, not the
# profile's.
#
# WHY THEY ARE STILL HERE: two-deploy retirement, as for MC_MAX_RECEIVE_SLOTS
# above: the rollback image reads them as REQUIRED. Refs `optional: true`;
# scheduled removal in docs/TODO.md §Media Path Obligations, "Delete the
# story-3 retired config keys".
MC_AUDIO_CODEC=opus
MC_AUDIO_MAX_BITRATE_BPS=48000
MC_AUDIO_FRAME_RATE_HZ=50
# ==========================================================================
# Meeting KEK lifecycle (ADR-0036 §4 Rotation, "Participant leaves") — story
# 2026-09-21-hear-each-other R-12; manifest story task 4, read by MC task 9.
#
# W: the KEK rotation debounce window, in seconds. Every roster removal
# schedules a rotation, debounced to at most once per W measured from the
# OLDEST un-rotated removal. R-12 states the leaver exposure bound AS W.
#
# REQUIRED, NO RUST DEFAULT — the same precedent, for the same reason, as the
# client-facing media signalling keys above: a Rust-defaulted key wired
# into mc-0 and forgotten on mc-1 passes `dt-guard env-config` green while the
# two instances behave differently. For W that divergence is a SECURITY
# defect, not a tuning one: the leaver exposure bound becomes whichever pod
# owns the meeting, and nobody outside can tell which. The documented default
# is the value below — this file is its home, not a constant in config.rs.
#
# W REACHES EVERY PARTICIPANT. MC carries it on the wire as
# `kek_rotation_debounce_seconds` on JoinResponse and MeetingKekUpdate, and
# clients derive their previous-KEK retention from it. The rule lives at
# `JoinResponse.kek_rotation_debounce_seconds` in
# proto/dark_tower/signaling/v1/signaling.proto and is deliberately not
# restated here. So tuning W moves client key hygiene too, not just a server
# timer; and an already-connected client keeps the old W until its next
# MeetingKekUpdate, so server-side rotation and client-side retention disagree
# for a bounded interval by construction.
#
# ORDERING: the ROLLOUT ORDERING / COUPLED SET IS THREE ARTIFACTS block above
# governs this key unchanged (this ConfigMap + BOTH Deployments + the image,
# applied with-or-before each other). The W-specific consequence: a VALUE EDIT
# of W here renames the content-addressed ConfigMap and ROLLS both MC pods, so
# every meeting on them restarts and the new W is in force once the rollout
# completes. Never remove this key in the same deploy that removes its read —
# that is a CrashLoop. (Corrected 2026-10-20 from "a later value edit does not
# roll the pods", which ADR-0038 §2 made false.)
# Enforced by: mc-service, at config load (ConfigError::MissingEnvVar if
# absent; rejects a value outside the bound). The bound's figures and their
# derivation live in crates/mc-service/src/config.rs
# (MIN_/MAX_KEK_ROTATION_DEBOUNCE_SECONDS), deliberately not restated here.
# A missing key is also loud at the Kubernetes layer: both Deployments
# reference it with a non-optional configMapKeyRef, so a pod without it gets
# CreateContainerConfigError.
# Where an operator sees it: mc_meeting_kek_rotation_window_seconds (W as the
# pod loaded it — it must equal the value below on every MC pod), beside
# mc_meeting_kek_rotation_pending_age_seconds and
# mc_meeting_kek_generated_total{trigger}. docs/runbooks/mc-deployment.md
# §Configuration Reference carries its row.
MC_KEK_ROTATION_DEBOUNCE_SECONDS=60
# ---- Connect settle window (ADR-0036 §9; story 2 task 20) ----
# How long MC waits, after the FIRST media-handler connection MH reports for a
# participant, before routing on whatever connections it has observed. It ends
# EARLY the moment the participant is connected to every handler of its
# meeting, so a participant that reaches all handlers pays nothing.
# NOT A DEBOUNCE — do not remove or shrink it to "speed things up". Edges never
# move while both parties can still use them, so without the window a
# participant whose second handler connect lands ~100 ms after its first is
# routed on ONE handler and stays there, and in a fully-connected meeting every
# sender ends up sending to both handlers permanently.
# Derivation of 1500: ~15x the observed gap between one client's parallel
# handler connects as MH reports them in Kind (~100 ms), and it must also cover
# the first-join RegisterMeeting fan-out to every handler — MH only reports a
# connection once the meeting is registered on that handler, so a window
# shorter than that fan-out re-creates the split this window exists to
# prevent. Bounded 100..=10000 at load: below ~100 ms it stops doing its job;
# above 10 s it is a long, silent delay to first audio for anyone who cannot
# reach every handler.
# Cost: time-to-first-audio for a participant that does NOT reach every
# handler is delayed by up to this window. It never appears in join latency
# (mc_session_join_duration_seconds stops at the JoinResponse).
# Enforced by: mc-service, in-process, in the meeting actor. Required, no Rust
# default: a pod without the key crash-loops on MissingEnvVar.
# Where an operator sees it: mc_media_connect_settle_window_seconds (the value
# the pod enforces) and mc_media_connect_settles_total{outcome="window_elapsed"}
# (participants that settled without reaching every handler).
# ORDERING: the ROLLOUT ORDERING / COUPLED SET IS THREE ARTIFACTS block above
# governs this key unchanged. A VALUE EDIT here rolls mc-0 and mc-1 (the
# ConfigMap is content-addressed); check mc_media_connect_settle_window_seconds
# for the window each pod enforces. (Corrected 2026-10-20 from "a later value
# edit does not roll the pods".)
MC_MEDIA_CONNECT_SETTLE_MS=1500

# ---- Media-handler heartbeat timeout, N (ADR-0039 §1; story 4 MC-B1) ----
# MC's ONE heartbeat value. MC sends each MH the interval floor(N/3), minimum
# 1 ms, per meeting in RegisterMeetingRequest.heartbeat_interval_ms (MH has no
# cadence config, so the two ends cannot drift), and judges handler liveness
# against N (detection lands in MC-B2).
# Range: (0, 30000] ms; default 6000. Legal but expensive below ~1500 ms:
# every (meeting, handler) pair sends one heartbeat per interval into its
# meeting actor, so load is meetings x handlers / (N/3) messages per second.
# Do not lower it without load testing. Always far below MH's owner-silence
# release (MH_MEETING_OWNER_SILENCE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS, [300, 3600] s).
# Enforced by: mc-service, at startup (out of range or missing refuses boot;
# REQUIRED, no Rust default — a pod without the key crash-loops on
# MissingEnvVar, so apply this ConfigMap WITH or BEFORE the image).
# Where an operator sees it: mc_media_handler_heartbeat_deadline_seconds (the
# N each pod enforces) and mc_media_handler_heartbeat_interval_seconds.
# A VALUE EDIT here rolls mc-0 and mc-1 (the ConfigMap is content-addressed).
# The browser's replacement caps (media.handlers.reconnectingCapMs /
# resumeAbandonMs in packages/sdk-core/src/config/clientConfig.ts) are sized on
# N; raising N fails packages/web-app/tests/replacementCapsSync.test.ts until
# those caps are raised too (else every ordinary crash reads as `abandoned`).
MC_MH_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_MS=6000

# ---- media_dial credential TTL (ADR-0039 §5; story 4 MC-D) ----
# Lifetime, in seconds, of the media_dial token MC mints per (participant,
# handler) and puts on every MediaHandlerSet entry. MC re-pushes each
# participant's set every TTL/3 (each participant up to a tenth of that EARLIER,
# jittered, never later), so a held token expires only after ~3 consecutive
# dropped pushes. A token is reusable for its whole TTL, so the TTL also bounds
# how long a departed participant's token stays signature-valid (MH then
# declines it at binding: MC answers sender_id 0).
# Range: [60, 900] s; shipped 300. Lower = more signing + push work per meeting
# (participants x handlers / (TTL/3)); higher = longer token reuse window.
# Enforced by: mc-service, at startup (out of range, unparseable or missing
# refuses boot; REQUIRED, no Rust default — apply this ConfigMap WITH or BEFORE
# the image). Read once at boot: a change restarts the pods, which ends their
# meetings (and their per-meeting dial keys).
# Where an operator sees it: mc_media_dial_token_ttl_seconds and
# mc_media_dial_token_refresh_interval_seconds.
# A VALUE EDIT here rolls mc-0 and mc-1 (the ConfigMap is content-addressed).
MC_MEDIA_DIAL_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS=300

# ---- Media-handler replacement pacing (ADR-0039 §3; story 4 MC-C) ----
# After MC removes a dead or impaired handler (removal never waits on GC), it
# asks GC for ONE replacement per meeting at a time (AcquireMediaHandler).
# MC_MH_REPLACEMENT_MIN_INTERVAL_MS is the per-meeting minimum interval between
# attempts AND the backoff ladder's first step; MC_MH_REPLACEMENT_BACKOFF_MAX_MS
# caps the ladder (it doubles per failed attempt: no capacity / GC unavailable).
# Retries use earlier-only jitter in [step/2, step], never below the minimum.
# There is NO per-MC limiter (user ruling 2026-10-31): a crashed handler's
# meetings all ask at once, so LOWERING the minimum raises GC's Acquire rate in
# a mass handler loss (meetings x attempts); RAISING the cap lengthens recovery
# of a meeting awaiting a handler.
# Ranges: minimum [100, 60000] ms (default 1000); cap [minimum, 300000] ms
# (default 30000; a cap below the minimum refuses boot).
# Derived, never configured: the replacement retry budget = the ladder up to
# its first capped step + one capped step + the push worker's
# initial-registration bound (139 s at these defaults), published as
# mc_media_handler_replacement_retry_budget_seconds; the awaiting-handler page
# bound derives from it, so editing either key moves the page.
# Enforced by: mc-service, at startup (missing, unparseable or out of range
# refuses boot; REQUIRED, no Rust default — apply this ConfigMap WITH or BEFORE
# the image). An older MC image ignores both keys, so an image-only rollback is
# safe.
# Where an operator sees it: mc_media_handler_replacement_min_interval_seconds,
# mc_media_handler_replacement_backoff_max_seconds and the budget gauge above.
# A VALUE EDIT here rolls mc-0 and mc-1 (the ConfigMap is content-addressed).
MC_MH_REPLACEMENT_MIN_INTERVAL_MS=1000
MC_MH_REPLACEMENT_BACKOFF_MAX_MS=30000

# ---- Planned-transfer cutover margin (ADR-0039 §4/§7; story 4 MC-F) ----
# When a media handler drains (SIGTERM: mh.sh drain, upgrade, scale-down) it
# reports DRAINING and drain_remaining_ms on its per-meeting heartbeat, keeps
# forwarding, and sheds what it still holds at its own deadline = SIGTERM + MH
# terminationGracePeriodSeconds (infra/services/mh-service/deployment.yaml) −
# MH's shutdown margin (published as mh_drain_window_seconds). MC moves each
# meeting make-before-break and CUTS OVER at that deadline − this margin:
# stragglers are cut over (outcome timed_out). The margin must cover the set
# push, the old handler's worker drain and the EndMeeting round trip, so MH
# releases by EndMeeting rather than by its deadline shed.
# Relation: keep it well below mh_drain_window_seconds minus one heartbeat
# interval; at or above the MH drain window every planned transfer degrades to
# an immediate cutover (timed_out). Guarded by env-test 02.
# Range: [1000, 30000] ms (default 5000).
# Enforced by: mc-service, at startup (missing, unparseable or out of range
# refuses boot; REQUIRED, no Rust default — apply this ConfigMap WITH or BEFORE
# the image). An older MC image ignores the key, so an image-only rollback is
# safe.
# Where an operator sees it: mc_media_handler_planned_transfer_margin_seconds.
# A VALUE EDIT here rolls mc-0 and mc-1 (the ConfigMap is content-addressed).
MC_PLANNED_TRANSFER_MARGIN_MS=5000

# ---- Client-loss disagreement threshold (ADR-0039 §8; story 4 MC-E) ----
# OBSERVATIONAL ONLY: client loss reports never trigger a removal in this
# story, so tuning these changes only mc_media_handler_client_loss_disagreements_total.
# A (meeting, handler) disagrees when at least FRACTION of the clients MC
# believes connected to it (MH-reported) AND at least MIN_CLIENTS of them
# report losing it, for longer than N (MC_MH_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_MS), while its
# heartbeats keep it Live: the false-negative half of the client-loss evidence
# (it detects a forged heartbeat ONLY when the forger echoes the live
# connection ids: an empty or wrong list retires the believed-connected set
# through the heartbeat reconcile, so the counter stays 0 — zero is NOT
# evidence of no forgery). Client-asserted: forgeable by
# >= MIN_CLIENTS colluding participants, so not a trust signal.
# Range: FRACTION finite in (0, 1] (default 0.5); MIN_CLIENTS integer in
# [1, 1000] (default 3).
# Enforced by: mc-service, at startup (missing, unparseable or out of range
# refuses boot; REQUIRED, no Rust default — apply this ConfigMap WITH or BEFORE
# the image). An older MC image ignores the keys, so an image-only rollback is
# safe.
# Where an operator sees them: mc_media_handler_client_loss_disagreement_ratio
# and mc_media_handler_client_loss_disagreement_min_clients.
# A VALUE EDIT here rolls mc-0 and mc-1 (the ConfigMap is content-addressed).
MC_CLIENT_LOSS_DISAGREEMENT_FRACTION=0.5
MC_CLIENT_LOSS_DISAGREEMENT_MIN_CLIENTS=3

# ---- Capacity caps (R-37, story 4 MC-A) ----
# MC_MAX_MEETINGS: concurrent meetings this MC accepts (GC's AssignMeetingWithMh
# is refused at_capacity beyond it). MC_MAX_PARTICIPANTS: participants across
# all its meetings. Both are the load-shed levers the incident runbooks name.
# Enforced by: mc-service, at load. REQUIRED, no Rust default: a pod without
# either key, or with an unparseable value or 0, refuses boot (crash-loops
# with the key named). Before story 4 MC-A a typo here silently fell back to
# the default, which made a mistyped load-shed lever invisible. CHECK THE
# VALUE BEFORE ROLLING: a malformed value CrashLoops the new pod (the old one
# keeps serving under RollingUpdate).
# Where an operator sees it: the values are sent to GC on RegisterMC and
# logged at MC startup.
MC_MAX_MEETINGS=1000
MC_MAX_PARTICIPANTS=10000

# ---- Meeting lifetime timers (join-window devloop, 2026-11-12) ----
# MC_MEETING_JOIN_WINDOW_SECONDS — a meeting no participant joins within this
# long of its creation (GC's assignment) ends through the same path as an
# emptied one (cause=empty, reason=join_window_expired; GC is notified).
# PRODUCTION VALUE below (operator direction 2026-11-13): short enough that a
# never-joined assignment (an abandoned GC join) holds MC capacity, key material
# and its GC row only briefly, long enough to sit well above the GC-join ->
# MC-admission latency of a real client. A join that loses that race is refused
# and the SDK re-joins through GC (bounded JOIN_RETRY), so the window is not a
# join-correctness bound — BUT keep it well above the p99 client GC-join ->
# MC-admission latency: a client consistently slower than the window fails
# EVERY retry (each retry gets a fresh meeting with the same window). Symptom:
# join_window_expired rising together with
# mc_session_join_failures_total{error_type=meeting_not_found|teardown_in_progress};
# remedy: raise this value.
# Bounds (actors/meeting_timers.rs): floor LIFETIME_TICK, ceiling the DERIVED
# default (meeting-token TTL cap + JWT exp leeway + SDK join deadline) — which
# is also what MC runs if this key is ever removed.
# Enforced by: mc-service, at startup (outside [floor, ceiling] or unparseable
# refuses boot); read by BOTH mc-N-deployment.yaml via an optional ref.
# Kind overrides it for the Layer 7 budget
# (infra/kubernetes/overlays/kind/services/mc-service/configmap-join-window-patch.yaml).
# Where an operator sees it: mc_meeting_join_window_seconds.
# Raising it does NOT fix clients that cannot reach MC (see MCJoinsNotReachingMC).
# A VALUE EDIT here rolls mc-0 and mc-1 (the ConfigMap is content-addressed).
MC_MEETING_JOIN_WINDOW_SECONDS=30
#
# MC_DISCONNECT_GRACE_PERIOD_SECONDS — reconnect grace for an abruptly
# disconnected participant (ADR-0023; Rust default 30). Enforced by every
# meeting actor. Where an operator sees it: mc_meeting_disconnect_grace_seconds.
